+---
+id: dl-003-json-patch-document-and-values-must-be-json-values-move-to-the-same-location-still-requires-its-source
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Patch: document and values must be JSON values; move to the same location still requires its source"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Users of `applyPatch` (dl-002-json-patch-api-atomic-copy-on-apply-upfront-validation-jsonpatcherror-with-index)
+reported patches they consider invalid being accepted and failures that do not match RFC 6902. A review
+against the RFC found:
+
+- `move` with `from` equal to `path` returned the document unchanged without checking that `from`
+  exists, although RFC 6902 section 4.4 says "The "from" location MUST exist for the operation to be
+  successful" (e.g. `{"op":"move","from":"/x","path":"/x"}` on `{}` succeeded).
+- RFC 6902 operates on JSON documents and its `value` members are JSON values (sections 3, 4), but
+  `applyPatch` accepted any JavaScript value and silently changed it while copying: `NaN`/`Infinity`
+  became numbers no JSON text holds, a `Date` or `Map` became `{}`, a function or nested `undefined`
+  vanished, and a cyclic value crashed with a `RangeError` instead of a `JsonPatchError`. The same
+  held for the document.
+- An operation was accepted when its `op` was inherited from a prototype, or when it was an instance
+  of a class (`Map`, `Date`), unlike `path`, `from` and `value`, which already had to be own members.
+- A hole in a sparse patch array was skipped by `Array.prototype.map`, so it was silently ignored
+  instead of rejected as a non-operation.
+
+## Decision
+
+1. **`move` onto its own location resolves `from` first**: it still changes nothing, but fails like any
+   `move` whose `from` does not exist (including `-` and out-of-range indexes).
+2. **Only JSON values are accepted.** A JSON value is `null`, a boolean, a finite number, a string, an
+   array without holes or a plain object (prototype `Object.prototype` or `null`) made of JSON values,
+   with no cycle; a sub-value shared by several places, without a cycle, is accepted and copied for
+   each place. A `value` member that is not one makes the operation invalid (`JsonPatchError` with its
+   `index`, reported during the upfront validation of dl-002 point 2). A document that is not one
+   throws a `JsonPatchError` whose `index` is `undefined`, checked after the patch is validated.
+   Unknown members of an operation are still ignored, whatever they hold (RFC 6902 section 4).
+3. **An operation must be a plain object with an own `op`**, like `path`, `from` and `value`.
+4. **Every element of the patch array is an operation**: holes are validated, and rejected, too.
+5. Unchanged, as decided in dl-002: `remove` of the root stays an error, and the whole patch is still
+   validated before any operation is applied.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Every failure of `applyPatch` is a `JsonPatchError`; no input is silently altered.
+- Callers that passed non-JSON values (a `Date`, `undefined` as the document) now get an error and
+  must serialise those values themselves, as sending the patch as JSON would have done.
+- Validating the document costs one more traversal of it per call, the same order as the copy.
+- Objects from another realm (e.g. `vm`) have another `Object.prototype` and are rejected; callers
+  parse JSON in their own realm.
