@@ -0,0 +1,34 @@
+---
+id: task-003-make-applypatch-conform-to-rfc-6902-in-every-case-errors-included
+type: task
+title: "Make applyPatch conform to RFC 6902 in every case, errors included"
+status: pending
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Users of `applyPatch` report surprising results: some patches they consider invalid are accepted, some
+edge cases give unexpected results, and a failing patch does not always behave as they read the
+standard. Review how patches are checked and applied, and make the behaviour conform to RFC 6902 in
+every case, errors included.
+
+## What was done
+
+- Reviewed `applyPatch` against RFC 6902 sections 3 to 5 and appendix A. Found and fixed (see
+  dl-003-json-patch-document-and-values-must-be-json-values-move-to-the-same-location-still-requires-its-source):
+  - `move` with `from` equal to `path` succeeded when `from` did not exist (section 4.4);
+  - non-JSON values (`NaN`, `Infinity`, `BigInt`, symbols, functions, `Date`, `Map`, nested
+    `undefined`, sparse arrays) in a `value` or the document were accepted and silently altered,
+    and a cyclic value crashed with a `RangeError`;
+  - an operation with an inherited `op`, or that is a class instance, was accepted;
+  - a hole in a sparse patch array was skipped instead of rejected.
+- Checked and left unchanged (conforming, or approved in dl-002): pointer resolution, `-` and index
+  bounds, `replace`/`remove` requiring an existing target, the proper-prefix rule of `move`, `copy`
+  into its own child, `test` equality, ignored unknown members, atomicity, the root `remove` error.
+- `test/json-patch.test.ts`: 7 new tests written first; 5 failed (red), and the 2 for shared
+  sub-values and null-prototype objects are characterization tests that passed at once.
+- `src/index.ts`: `isPlainObject` and `isJsonValue`; validation of operations, values and the
+  document; `move` onto itself resolves `from`; doc comments updated.
+- `README.md`: the JSON-value requirement and the new error cases.
+- `npm test`: 52 tests pass. `tsc` is not installed here, so the type check was not run.
